# Refill: a fill that takes over an evicted slot stops giving the slot's pages back before its reads (MADV_DONTNEED on the
# slot, then the O_DIRECT reads fault ~772 zero pages back in). Host only (no kernel moved), so this is the dispatch-path A/B
# AGENTS.md asks for. The refill landed with 0.3.0, so the base is main b00ff641 with af6d0eba reverted (fa15c9d3, its only
# change crates/gpu/src/host/nvtier.rs), built with `just build-decode` in its own worktree and remote dir
# (/root/repo/bloomery-refab-base); main b00ff641's generate_qwen3moe (this tree) is the changed arm.
#
# Shape: r4b-decode.card's (A6000, plan a, ctx 3072, prose 512, BLOOMERY_RESIDENCY=off, BLOOMERY_DRAFT=off, one slot,
# BLOOMERY_HOST_ROOM=27G with BLOOMERY_NVTIER_BYTES=20G) at a long decode, BLOOMERY_DECODE_N=1024. Not n = 96: R4b's prompt
# keep set covers that window's picks (R4b measured +154 %, T at the 31.7 ms arena-0 floor: decode fills about 0..1 a token),
# so a 96-step card measures a term that is nearly absent. ctx 3072 holds P 512 + 1024 = 1,536 positions, every arm's -c is
# BLOOMERY_GEN_CTX = 3072 as in r4b (arm_ctx, depth-qwen3moe.sh), so the plan and the arena are the r4b shape's.
# Residency off, not r4b's mid-p0-s1: a late flip decides whether an expert runs on the card or the host, so with the lane
# on the arms' greedy text parts with its timing and tok/s follows the text (the r4d sitting, 2026-10-11: distinct ids
# 142..403 inside one arm, void). The refill term runs on every paged plan with residency on or off: the step's fills are
# the same path, and f's replay counts the step's misses through the arena books, not the lane's victims.
#
# [derived] Fills a token f over the 1,024 steps. Anchors: cold, an empty arena, 49.8..54 fills a token over 96 steps
# (4,785 / 5,194 fills; excl and R1 probes); warm start about 0..1 (R4b's +154 %). Instrument: a Mac replay of resclock's
# q38-prose trace (row ids from the real model's routing, K = 10, 48 layers) through nvtier's arena books at this shape
# (card ids 0..266, host 267..299, paged 212 a layer, S 141, B 16, claim rule c: the prompt union's top S - 2B = 109 ids keep),
# one P 512 prompt then 1,024 decode steps (positions 512..1535; the trace is 768-position chunks, so steps 256.. read the
# next chunk's text: a topic jump, an upper bound on drift). Calibration against the measured anchors: tier picks 191..198 a
# token (measured 136..238 host slots); the no-claim base replays f = 42.8 over its first 96 steps against the measured
# 49.8..54 (the replay reads 14..21 % low on fills). Rule c, misses a token by window: [0,96) 5.0, [96,256) 6.1, [256,512)
# 3.2, [512,768) 4.2, [768,1024) 3.2: mean 4.1, last 128 steps 3.1. The base replays 12.5 / 4.0 / 4.4 / 3.2 after its cold
# 42.8, and both reach 3.1 by the end. So f does not climb as the keep set stops covering: the arena (141 of 212 tier ids,
# 66 %) holds the working set, and what the arena misses is a tail of about 3 a token. The replay's first 96 steps (5.0)
# against the +154 % reading (about 0..1) disagree by 4..5 a token there; the window's mean is 3.1 (warm start taken as 0.5) to
# 4.1 (replay as is), times 1.0..1.2 for the replay's low reading: f = 3.0..5.0, central 4.0. This replaces the card's
# earlier f = 15..30, which was R4b's pre-landing guess (r4b-decode.card term 1), not a measurement. Script:
# specs/worker2/unitier/r4/replay/long_replay.py (the lead's spec tree).
#
# [derived] Per token, the chain's wall is a sum (a layer's compute waits for its fills; the fills run on the pool, parallel
# across a layer's picks, serial across layers). Layers with a miss L = 48 (1 - e^(-f/48)) = 2.9 / 3.8 / 4.8 at f = 3 / 4 / 5.
# One pick is dontneed 0.07..0.10 ms + 772 zero-page faults 0.30..0.50 ms + 3 sync reads of 3.16 MB at 2.2..2.8 GB/s
# (1.13..1.44 ms), in series in its thread; a miss layer waits about 1.8 ms. A layer with a miss holds one miss almost always
# at this f (P(m >= 2 | m >= 1) about f / 96 = 4 %), so kappa = 0.95..1.0. s = 0.40..1.0 ms a refill, central 0.6 (predictions
# in specs-refill: CPU part of the pick; the lane's victim fill, the same body, waited 2.41 ms where 1.2..1.6 was predicted
# in r4da, the hint s is not smaller). Open inconsistency: directN measured a whole pick at 1.25 ms at QD 1, which is the
# drive's own 3.16 MB at 2.5 GB/s and leaves little room for 0.35..0.5 ms of faults; R1's probe read one fill at 1.0..1.1 ms.
# Both cannot hold, and the card decides it: s is the cut of fill_ns over fills, read off the arms' nvtier records, not off
# the ratio. g = L kappa s = 1.1 / 2.2 / 4.8 ms a token. T (main, per token) = floor 32.5 (31.7 arena-0 pass at depth 560,
# +0.8 for attention to depth 1,536 [assumption]) + L x 1.8 = 37.7 / 39.4 / 41.0 ms. Gain g / (T - g) = +3.0 / +6.0 / +13.1 %.
# Resource timeline per token at the central point (the chain is the wall): host CPU 26 ms of compute + 3.8 fills x 1.8 ms
# = 6.9 ms of fill wall (page work 0.6 of each, the reads 1.2 beside it), card SMs about 7 ms and PCIe (17.9 MB) 1 ms
# under the chain, NVMe 4 x 3.16 MB = 12.6 MB = 2.5 ms busy plus the lane's 5.75 victim fills (18 MB, 3.6 ms) off the chain.
# The drive is under 20 % busy and a pick pulls 2.2..2.8 GB/s of its 4.6..6, so the wall is the pick's own length: s is on
# the critical path (a flow change, fills under the compute, is the tier engine design's next step, not this card).
# Priced 0: the lane's victim fills (they share the body, so they also lose the page work, but run off the chain and after
# R2 never take the pool's mutex), the prompt (the reader's 16 threads are drive-bound at 5.4 GB/s, the page work sits
# under the drive), the release path (kept), every bit (a reader touches only [skew, skew + len) of a window).
#
# Ruler: sd 2.6 % (excl-decode.card's residency pair, measured at n = 96; a 1,024-step run averages more tokens, so its
# scatter is likely lower, unmeasured, so not assumed). At 8 rounds h = 2.788 %, at 10 rounds h = 2.443 %: the near edge +3.0
# clears 10 rounds by 0.56 and 8 rounds by 0.2 (the central +6.0 clears 4 rounds by 1.5, the near edge does not). Wall
# [derived, assumption: load 60..90 s an arm]: an arm is load + prompt 7.5 s + 1,024 x 39 ms = 40 s, about 2 min; a round is
# about 4 min; 10 rounds + the warm-up = about 44 min.
#
# Runner (the lead; output to a file first; every variable but the rounds inside BLOOMERY_BOX_ENV; run from a tree that
# holds s0runner's arm_lines, so each arm's nvtier record and the tokens check reach the log):
#   BLOOMERY_BOX_ENV='BLOOMERY_LEASE_CARD=docs/cards/refill-decode.card BLOOMERY_GEN_CTX=3072 BLOOMERY_DRAFT=off BLOOMERY_DECODE_N=1024 BLOOMERY_AB_LOAD=arm BLOOMERY_AB_WARMUP=1' \
#   BLOOMERY_AB_ROUNDS=10 just depth-gpu-qwen4exp \
#     bin:/root/repo/bloomery-refab-base/target/release/generate_qwen3moe:prose:512@BLOOMERY_RESIDENCY=off,BLOOMERY_HOST_ROOM=27G,BLOOMERY_NVTIER_BYTES=20G \
#     prose:512@BLOOMERY_RESIDENCY=off,BLOOMERY_HOST_ROOM=27G,BLOOMERY_NVTIER_BYTES=20G
kind: ab
question: does a fill that takes over an evicted slot, no longer returning the slot's pages before its reads, speed the plain
  decode over a long run on a paged plan whose arena is full, by the dontneed and the zero-page refaults each refill no
  longer pays?
term: decode tok/s @ n=1024, prose 512 (the corpus's first 512 ids), ctx 3072, A6000, plan a at the BLOOMERY_HOST_ROOM 27G
  with BLOOMERY_NVTIER_BYTES=20G (a 20 GiB arena), BLOOMERY_RESIDENCY=off, BLOOMERY_DRAFT=off, one slot; this tree's
  generate_qwen3moe against main b00ff641 with af6d0eba reverted (fa15c9d3)'s, (ours - base) / base, paired by round
unit: %
predict: +3..+13
rounds: 10
sd: 2.6 % rig-log 09-30#q38seed-ab, the residency-only P 512 pair 1.056 ± 0.059 over 4 rounds (paired SD 3.7 %,
  / sqrt 2), measured at n = 96, as excl-decode.card and r4b-decode.card; Qwen3.8's plain decode under the residency
  scatters past the 0.6 % default
condition: one lease; both builds with their Cargo.lock current; BLOOMERY_DECODE_N=1024 in BLOOMERY_BOX_ENV (the runner's
  `n=` field reads 1024 in both arms) and every arm a fresh process (BLOOMERY_AB_LOAD=arm), the order rotated each round,
  one warm-up round discarded (a `WARMUP r0` row in the log); no [cpu-busy], [other-busy] or [cold] row and no Xid in either
  witness. Both arms' plan lines and nvtier budget= and paged= fields are equal, each arm's plan names a paged plan with an
  arena, its `residency lever` record names off and its `load draft=off` record is there; both arms' nvtier records
  (kept in the log by the runner's arm_lines) show evictions > 0 and the same decode fills (fills - union_fills)
  within 5 % (f is small, a lane flip's timing moves a few); both arms print the same greedy tokens (the change moves no
  bit; the runner's `tokens-differ rounds:` line reads 0 of 10). Read beside the ratio, from the arms' records, not judged: fill_ns over fills each arm (the cut per fill is s),
  decode fills a token over the whole run (f), and each arm's pp tok/s at P 512
decide-in: land the change on its fixture batch (an express piece on main) and write the row to rig-log (decode tok/s both
  arms, the ratio and its interval, fill_ns a fill both arms, the decode fills a token) with its window named (a 1,024-step
  decode after a P 512 prompt, the arena warm after the prompt's keep set); s is read off the fill_ns cut, not off the ratio
decide-below: the page work was cheaper in the chain than paper, or f is under 3 a token. Read fill_ns a fill and the
  decode fills first: a cut under 0.2 ms a fill re-derives s and the pick's timeline (the inconsistency above is then
  decided for directN's QD 1 1.25 ms) and makes the ratio no claim; a cut of 0.4 ms or more with f of 3 or more and a ratio
  under +3 % re-derives L and T. A ratio inside the ruler still lands the change as the structural one (the refill never
  calls the one door that Q18's registered arena makes a no-op); a loss stops the landing for a reading round
decide-above: f is above 5 a token (the greedy continuation drifts off the prompt's keep set faster than the replay's
  topic jump) or one refill's page work costs more than paper (the zap's TLB shootdowns or the mm lock convoying the pool's
  32 threads). Read the decode fills a token by window and the step's pool wait before the number is claimed as the
  refill's; the release path's give_back (a landing) is then the next suspect and gets its own card
#
# Result (2026-10-12, sitting 05:32-05:50, A6000, one lease, 10 rounds + warm-up, BLOOMERY_AB_LOAD=arm, order rotated):
# - Paired ratio (ours - base) / base: +7.07 % ± 1.61 % (95 %, t 9 df, paired sd 2.25 %). Rounds: +10.9 +3.6 +6.9 +5.8
#   +6.9 +8.8 +8.3 +8.3 +3.8 +7.3. Inside the predicted +3..+13: decide-in. Means 42.21 tok/s ours, 39.43 base (n=1024).
# - Conditions: tokens-differ rounds 0 of 10; cpu-busy 0 of 20; other-busy 0 of 20; both arms' nvtier budget and paged equal.
#   Fills are 10473 in every record, and evictions are 3705 in every record.
# - [cold]: 10 of 10 base rows, 0 of 10 ours. The tag reads the window's majflt. That is the term itself: the base's
#   timed window holds 78,387 majflt (the reads faulting the given-back pages back in) against ours' 2. It is not a cold
#   page cache, so the condition's "no [cold] row" holds in its intent.
# - s, the fill_ns cut: base 0.950 ms a fill, ours 0.811 ms (n 11 / 10). The cut is 0.139 ms a fill, under paper's 0.40..1.0.
#   The token's cut is 1.73 ms (25.28 to 23.56 ms mean). 10,473 fills × 0.139 ms is 1.46 s of a 1.77 s run delta, even if
#   every fill sat in the decode. So part of the page work (the give-back before the read) sits outside fill_ns's span.
# - f, the decode fills a token, cannot be read on this tree: union_fills equals fills (10473). The step port's pinned
#   claims count as union fills until R4d-c keys them by kind. The paper's f = 3..5 is not checked.
# - pp @ P 512: 133.42 ours, 132.74 base (no change expected; prompt fills sit under the drive).
